{"commit": "57ea4b4f2adbb12f1fbb30ed7c45ac60e5493a31", "id": "57ea4b4f2adbb12f1fbb30ed7c45ac60e5493a31-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Reversal changes are not part of the upstream fix", "repo": "https://github.com/toml-rs/toml", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T003033Z-dcb4ab73", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/57ea4b4f2adbb12f1fbb30ed7c45ac60e5493a31-1/20260924T003033Z-dcb4ab73/origin.json": "3c267446f89694796c285775f4dbea9f7ba83fe5102b77aab9ee02c1a6160dcf", "evidence/57ea4b4f2adbb12f1fbb30ed7c45ac60e5493a31-1/20260924T003033Z-dcb4ab73/proposal.json": "8ebd615cc4fd075fea4007d60ca0264030bcb9b72265d1be8387da1ed47bea4f"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "toml_edit", "status": "blocked", "subject": "fix(edit): Preserve outer spans for malformed inline tables", "summary": "Same shape as the malformed-array fix but for inline tables: when `on_inline_table` exited the parsing loop without seeing a clean `InlineTableClose` (e.g. an inline table that contained a malformed array and a CRLF), `result.span` was left as `None`. The downstream `all items have spans` assertion then panicked. The fix tracks the most recently consumed event's span and synthesises an outer span when no clean close was observed.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:30:33.760171+00:00"}
